docs(whitepaper): patient expository page on how we prove game theory

Adds /whitepaper/how-we-prove-game-theory — a long-form public-facing page
for readers who took game theory once in college and never saw the
computational side. Walks the commitment problem from Hobbes/Hume/Schelling
to Aumann's correlated equilibrium (1974), pins how the Port Daddy daemon
plays the role of the trusted referee, then opens the hood on TLA+, TLC,
Apalache, and Z3 — what each does, what it actually closes, where the
honest gaps still are.

Structure: six sections with their own Mermaid figure, plus 7 right-gutter
sidenotes (Tufte-style), 5 pull-quotes, and a runnable six-line Z3 SMT
sample the reader can copy into their terminal. Includes a sketched
claim-signaling.tla artifact marked [planned, v2.6] — because the paper
today uses TLC + ProVerif + Kani + EasyCrypt + fast-check, not Apalache
or Z3 yet, and the page is candid about which proofs are landed vs.
planned. Limitations section names spec-faithfulness, tool trust, and
state explosion as the honest places to be skeptical.

Wires the route into main.tsx, adds a sibling tile on the whitepaper
landing page (now a 3-column grid on lg+), and a conditional tile on
the agent-transactions PaperDetailPage. SiteMetadata registration is
intentionally deferred until the OG-card generator is healthy upstream
(currently broken on a missing blog-control-plane.jpg from other WIP).

Validation: tsc --noEmit clean, vite build clean (49.65 kB chunk gzipped
to 14.45 kB), eslint clean. Vitest pass/fail count unchanged at 118/12 —
every pre-existing failure pre-dates this change. Voice audited against
user_voice_website.md: cathedral build, em-dash asides, cross-domain
analogies (four-way stops, Cardano, the kitchen chemist), self-deprecation
as ballast, no corporate evenness.


